This is a known result from the literature on nondeterministic state complexity of finite words (Carayol/Gordon-type "ambiguity" results): for a single word x of length n, the minimal NFA accepting exactly {x} among words of length n versus the minimal unambiguous NFA. The question asks for the least n where an NFA with unique accepting path can require more states than an unrestricted NFA.

I recall the result from Jeffrey Shallit and coauthors (or Kiefer/Okhotin style): for words of length n ≤ 4, the unambiguous and general nondeterministic state complexities coincide, and the first separation occurs at length 5. I will go with that.

