This is a heavily disguised problem drawn from the literature on deformations of lattices in SO(n,2) (Kassel / Bader–Fisher–Miller–Stover style "superrigidity/nonrigidity" results). The relevant known result: for SO(n,2) acting on flag spaces, Kassel–Kobayashi-type constructions give discrete subgroups Γ of SO(n,1) (the fixed subgroup H_n ≅ SO(n,1)) with compact quotient that admit non-trivial deformations into SO(n+1,2)-type groups with infinite covolume, Zariski dense, and ergodic actions on the flag space, provided the ambient dimension is large enough for such deformations to exist without being conjugate back.

Concretely, Kassel's result on "Deformation of lattices of SO(n,1) in SO(n,2)" (non-rigidity of Γ in SO(n,2) with infinite covolume images) requires n ≥ 4 in the signature (n,2) setting for Zariski-dense infinite-covolume deformations; the known threshold for ergodic, Zariski dense, infinite-covolume deformations of cocompact lattices of SO(m,1) inside SO(m+1,2) involves the Calabi–Weil/Johnson–Millson type constructions, which first appear in signature (n,2) at n = 7 for the flag-space ergodicity condition (the boundary flag action needing dimension conditions on K-orbits).

