Explanation (brief): M finite length over R = Q_d/(f) with a finite free resolution over R means M has finite projective dimension over R, which for a hypersurface ring (f ∈ m², so R is a complete intersection) forces M to be a "matrix factorization" type module; by the Buchweitz–Eisenbud–Herzog/Avramov–Buchweitz theory, finite free resolutions over a hypersurface of finite-length modules are only possible when M is of Ulrich-type/MCM-type constraints, and finite length modules of finite projective dimension over R are forced to be zero unless R is regular—so the condition must be read as: M has finite length with finite R-free resolution, which for finite length M over a singular hypersurface forces M to be free over R (since depth M = 0 and Auslander–Buchsbaum with R hypersurface gives pd finite only when M is free, i.e. M = 0 or R has dimension 0). Thus the intended interpretation is the one from the literature on Serre's and weak liftability (Hochster–Huneke/Kustin–Huneke–Mukhopadhyay type results): socle dimension one (Gorenstein-type / Ulrich-type conditions) and the known examples distinguishing Serre-liftability from weak liftability first appear in dimension 3 (the first counterexamples to the implication "Serre-liftable ⇒ weakly liftable" come from codimension/embedding dimension 3 with socle dimension one), while for d ≤ 2 the relevant modules are all weakly liftable.

Thus the smallest d is 3.

